A pull request event has arrived from the TaskFlow API repository.

  Action:    {{.action}}
  PR number: {{.number}}
  PR title:  {{.pull_request.title}}
  Branch:    {{.pull_request.head.ref}}
  URL:       {{.pull_request.html_url}}
  Author:    {{.pull_request.user.login}}

**If the fields above are blank**, this is a configuration fault, not an empty
event: the GitHub webhook is sending `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, so the
payload arrives as a single urlencoded `payload=` string and no field can be
read from it. Do not clone or search for something to review. Reply exactly:
`CONFIG ERROR: webhook content type must be application/json` and stop.

**Otherwise check the action.** If it is anything other than `opened`,
`reopened` or `synchronize`, this event does not need a review: reply exactly
`SKIPPED: <action>` and stop. Do not clone anything, do not review, do not post.
A closed or merged pull request in particular must never be reviewed.

Otherwise read your playbook and follow it exactly:

  cat /workspace/.agents/skills/review-a-taskflow-pr/SKILL.md

**This pull request is number {{.number}}.** Every bash command you run starts a
fresh shell, so an `export` does not carry over. Type `{{.number}}` literally
into every command that needs it — never rely on a shell variable holding it.

You are an independent reviewer: check the change out yourself, run the test
suite rather than trusting the PR body, and judge it against the numbered rules
in AGENTS.md. Post an APPROVE or REQUEST_CHANGES review with specific, cited
findings, then report the verdict back to the board.

Use your bash tool for every step and work from real command output — never
assess a diff you have not actually read.

Work autonomously; nobody is watching a terminal.
